      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224595",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/03/037f8ae326821862cf8e5a0206702c8583ce529964c375f3fc32d9494126c300.png)\n\nA speech therapist is testing a new method to treat dyslexia on a sample of $n = 60$ patients. Suppose that $x_{1}, x_{2}, \u2026, x_{60}$ are the scores of the patients in a dyslexia test after the treatment and $y_{1}, y_{2}, \u2026, y_{60}$ are the scores of the same patients before the treatment. It is known that the differences $X_{i} - Y_{i}$ between the scores after and before the treatment are I.I.D. random variables with population variance $\u03c3^{2} = 8^{2}$. You're given that the means for these samples are $x^(\u2015) = 72$ and $y^(\u2015) = 68$.\n\nFill in the missing information in the following statements.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 2.326) = 0.01$, where $Z\u223cN(0, 1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\nThe end-points of the $98%$ confidence interval for the difference between the average scores, rounded to two decimal places, are approximately\n$2$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nThe $98%$ confidence interval {{field-3}}.\nThis suggests that the average score after the treatment {{field-4}} the average score before the treatment.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
