      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251528",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/03/037f8ae326821862cf8e5a0206702c8583ce529964c375f3fc32d9494126c300.png)\n\nA tech company is evaluating the performance of a new processor on $n = 50$ computers. Suppose that $x_{1}, x_{2}, \u2026, x_{50}$ are the performance scores of the computers with the new processor and $y_{1}, y_{2}, \u2026, y_{50}$ are the scores of the computers with the old processor. It is known that the differences $X_{i} - Y_{i}$ are I.I.D random variables with population variance $\u03c3^{2} = 16^{2}$. You're given that the sample means are $x^(\u2015) = 255$ and $y^(\u2015) = 250$.\n\nFill in the missing information in the following statements.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 2.326) = 0.01$, where $Z\u223cN(0, 1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\nThe end-points of the $98%$ confidence interval for the difference between the average scores, rounded to two decimal places, are approximately\n$5$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nThe $98%$ confidence interval {{field-3}}.\nThis suggests that the average score of the new processor {{field-4}} the average score of the old processor.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
